umount.all -- System Administration

Unmount every filesystem except the root
/etc/umount.all

/etc/umount.all is a shell script run on the way to single-user mode by
/etc/shutdown.  It takes no arguments and no options.

It runs /etc/mount with no arguments to list what is mounted, and calls
/etc/umount on the block special device of every filesystem whose mount point
is not `/'.  The root is reported by mount like any other filesystem and is
recognised and skipped: it cannot be unmounted while the system is running.

A REFUSED UNMOUNT DOES NOT STOP THE REST.  umount(2) fails while any process
holds an open file or a working directory on the filesystem, and the ones that
are free should still come down, so each failure is discarded and the sweep
carries on.  When it is over, mount is run once more and anything still there
is named:

    umount.all: /dev/hd6 is still mounted on /usr

The exit status is always 0.  A caller that needs the answer as a status
should do its own unmounting: /etc/halt does, through umount(2), and reports
each refusal with the reason the kernel gave.

The sweep runs TWICE.  mount reports filesystems in the order /etc/rc mounted
them, which is shallowest first, and that is the wrong order for unmounting a
nested one -- /usr/man sits inside /usr, so /usr is busy until /usr/man is
gone.  The inner filesystem comes down on the first pass if it happened to be
reported first and on the second pass otherwise.

Files
/etc/mount	lists what is mounted, and does the unmounting
/etc/shutdown	the caller
/etc/mnttab	the table mount keeps

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it: the Lexicon documents
mount and umount, and never this script.  The page was written here, from the
script; none of Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

The script itself is a port of the COHERENT 4.2 original, which is generic
where the 3.x scripts of the same name were hand-written lists of one site's
own devices.  This shell has no comment lexer, so the script explains itself
in arguments to the `:' command.

